<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Miroslav of Hum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1199</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Miroslav (died c. 1199) was prince of Hum (Zachlumia), on the southern Adriatic coast, from the 1160s and a brother of the Serbian grand prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-nemanja">Stefan Nemanja</a>. He commissioned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miroslav-gospel">Miroslav Gospel</a>, one of the oldest and finest Serbian Cyrillic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, and built churches, among them that of Saints Peter and Paul at Bijelo Polje. He fought to defend his lands against Ragusa and the Bosnian ban.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/miroslav-of-hum.jpg" alt="Miroslav of Hum" width="320" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Miroslavs_Gospel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
